# Normalise line endings so they stop turning up in diffs.
#
# Git stores every text file with LF and converts on checkout, so it no longer
# matters whether an editor, a tool, or `dotnet sln add` writes CRLF or LF - the
# committed form is the same either way. Without this a file could appear
# rewritten in its entirety when only its line endings had changed.
#
# This cannot manage byte order marks: Git has no BOM handling at all. Visual
# Studio writes a BOM on some files and not others, and those are left as they
# are. A tool that strips one will still show up as a change to the first line.

* text=auto

# Windows tooling rewrites these with CRLF whenever it touches them, so keep
# CRLF in the working copy and avoid a diff every time they are saved.
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.csproj    text eol=crlf
*.props     text eol=crlf
*.targets   text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts need LF to stay runnable on Unix.
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Declared up front so none of these are ever normalised as text if added later.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.msi       binary
*.snk       binary
